{{\pard\plain \s3\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\adjustright \b\f1\cgrid 
Github Actions
\par}
{\tc\tcl3 \v Github Actions}
Three workflows under {\f2 .github/workflows/} :\par
{
\par
\pard\plain \s120\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls1\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls1\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
{\f2 cpp.yml}  - builds and tests (CMake configure + build Release + {\f2 ctest} ) on every push and pull request, on Windows and Ubuntu - intentionally lean, no report generation, so it stays fast and does not use many Actions minutes.\par
\pard\plain \s120\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls1\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls1\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
{\f2 pages.yml}  - on push to {\f2 main}  (and manually): builds the full report+site pipeline on both Windows and Ubuntu runners, merges both platforms' reports into one site, and deploys it to the {\f2 gh-pages}  branch (GitHub Pages). On a {\b private}  repository this step is skipped unless the repository variable {\f2 PAGES_ON_PRIVATE}  is {\f2 true}  (GitHub Pages needs GitHub Pro/Team, e.g. via the Student Developer Pack) - see docs/guide/releases.en.md.\par
\pard\plain \s120\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls1\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls1\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
{\f2 release.yml}  - on a {\f2 v*}  tag push (or manually): builds everything on both platforms and publishes a GitHub Release with every asset (Windows + Linux binaries, all reports, API docs, {\f2 site.zip} ). Prefer the local {\f2 10-release.bat} /{\f2 .sh}  script day to day; it does the same thing without using any Actions minutes.\par}
\par
\par}
{\bkmkstart AAAAAAAACP}
{\bkmkend AAAAAAAACP}
